<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sancho Ramírez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1042–<a href="/years/1094">1094</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Sancho V of Navarre, Sancho I of Aragon · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimenez-dynasty">Jiménez dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sancho Ramírez was King of Aragon from 1063 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>) from 1076 until his death in 1094, the son of Ramiro I. He placed Aragon under papal protection, introduced the Roman liturgy and pushed the frontier south against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. He died at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a>, which his son Peter I took in 1096.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sancho-ramirez.jpg" alt="Sancho Ramírez" width="320" height="162" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sancho Ramírez · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mancuso_de_Sancho_Ram%C3%ADrez.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sancho Ramírez was the eldest son of Ramiro I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ermesinda">Ermesinda</a> of Bigorre. His father was the first king of Aragon and an illegitimate son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho III of Pamplona</a>. Sancho Ramírez succeeded him as second king of Aragon in 1063. Like his father, he did not use the royal title early in his reign, even though his state had become fully independent. Between 1067 and 1068 the War of the Three Sanchos set him against his first cousins <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iv-of-navarre">Sancho IV of Navarre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-ii-of-castile">Sancho II of Castile</a>, who defeated the two allies and retook Bureba, Alta Rioja and Álava.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1076 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iv-of-navarre">Sancho IV of Navarre</a> was murdered by his own siblings, which began a succession crisis in the neighbouring kingdom. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> recognised the murdered king's young son García as titular king, while Sancho Ramírez gathered Navarrese nobles who resented Alfonso's influence. The crisis ended in partition: Sancho Ramírez was elected king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> and ceded the contested western provinces to Alfonso. From then he styled himself king of both Aragon and Navarre.</p><p>He conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbastro">Barbastro</a> in 1064, Graus in 1083 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monzon">Monzón</a> in 1089. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a>, who was raiding his lands and those of his Muslim allies, defeated him at the Battle of Morella, probably in 1084. Sancho died in 1094 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Sancho married first Isabella, daughter of Count Armengol III of Urgel, a marriage dissolved in 1071, and then Felicia, daughter of Hilduin IV, Count of Montdidier. His son Peter by Isabella succeeded him, followed by Alfonso and Ramiro, his sons by Felicia, so that three of his sons reigned in turn after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sancho Ramírez” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Sancho Ramírez</b> (r. 1063–1094) placed the kingdom under papal protection in 1068, introduced the Roman liturgy in 1071 and became king of Navarre as well in 1076, after his cousin Sancho IV was murdered.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Charters show his sons <b>Sancho Ramírez</b> and García Ramírez styled as kings, though little else is recorded.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-viguera">Kingdom of Viguera</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.14,-0.41&amp;year=1075" aria-label="Huesca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4540 664 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Sancho Ramírez"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4990 934h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4990 934h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=42.14,-0.41&amp;year=1075">What was here in 1075? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
